    {
      "id": "HmNbpoxQlfZSRM0pQUig3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2026.273957448963,
      "y": 713.253886714136,
      "width": 2355.518310546875,
      "height": 875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aB",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1856343152,
      "version": 3278,
      "versionNonce": 1775901296,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783489470543,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to improve booking experience by reserving tickets?\n- No one wants to spend 5 mins filling out a payment form and find out the tickets they wanted are no longer available because someone else booked it first.\n- A technique of a running timer is employed to solve this that reserves your ticket for that time period.\n- Need to ensure that the ticket is locked for user while they are checking out\n- Need to ensure if user abandons the checkout process, the ticket is released for other users\n- Need to ensure if user completed everything, ticket to be marked as sold and booking to be confirmed.\n\n1. Bad Solution : Long-Running DB Locks\n- A DB is directly used to lock a specific ticket row, done using SELECT FOR UPDATE is PostgreSQL.\n- The lock on the row is maintained until the txn is either committed or rolled back.\n- For unlocking:\n    - If txn committed DB lock is released, and ticket status set to BOOKED\n    - If user abandons, system relies on their subsequent actions or session timeouts to release the lock, has a risk of ticket being indefinitely locked if not properly handled.\n\nProblems:\n    - DB locks are designed for short period of time(a single-near instant txn)\n    - Keeping txn open for long period(5 mins) increases risk of lock-contention, deadlocks\n    - event though postgres support lock_timeout to fail txn that wait too long for locks, not a graceful solution because users will see an error rather than being queued.\n    - Requires application-level management and additional complexity\n    - Does not scales well under high load\n    - Crashes of DB/Application could not be handled well leaving locks in uncertain state.\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Status & Expiration Time with Cron\n- lock field by adding a status filed and expiration time on ticket table\n- Ticket States : available, reserved, booked.\n- When ticket selected by user ticket state available -> reserved, and timestamp recorded.\n- For unlocking : \n    - if purchase finalized, reserved -> booked, lock released.\n    - if abandoned, status changed back to available when expiration time reached, and lock released.\n    - To handle expiration time, we use a cron job to periodically query for rows with \"reserved\" status and expiration time reached, and set them back to \"available\"\n\nProblems:\n    - Delay in unlocking: there's a delay between ticket expiration and cron job trigger, this is not good specially for popular events and seat remains un-available even after expiration time till the cron resets it reducing booking opportunities.\n    - Reliability Issue : If cron job fails or has delays, more issues then, poor customer experience, revenue loss.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to improve booking experience by reserving tickets?\n- No one wants to spend 5 mins filling out a payment form and find out the tickets they wanted are no longer available because someone else booked it first.\n- A technique of a running timer is employed to solve this that reserves your ticket for that time period.\n- Need to ensure that the ticket is locked for user while they are checking out\n- Need to ensure if user abandons the checkout process, the ticket is released for other users\n- Need to ensure if user completed everything, ticket to be marked as sold and booking to be confirmed.\n\n1. Bad Solution : Long-Running DB Locks\n- A DB is directly used to lock a specific ticket row, done using SELECT FOR UPDATE is PostgreSQL.\n- The lock on the row is maintained until the txn is either committed or rolled back.\n- For unlocking:\n    - If txn committed DB lock is released, and ticket status set to BOOKED\n    - If user abandons, system relies on their subsequent actions or session timeouts to release the lock, has a risk of ticket being indefinitely locked if not properly handled.\n\nProblems:\n    - DB locks are designed for short period of time(a single-near instant txn)\n    - Keeping txn open for long period(5 mins) increases risk of lock-contention, deadlocks\n    - event though postgres support lock_timeout to fail txn that wait too long for locks, not a graceful solution because users will see an error rather than being queued.\n    - Requires application-level management and additional complexity\n    - Does not scales well under high load\n    - Crashes of DB/Application could not be handled well leaving locks in uncertain state.\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Status & Expiration Time with Cron\n- lock field by adding a status filed and expiration time on ticket table\n- Ticket States : available, reserved, booked.\n- When ticket selected by user ticket state available -> reserved, and timestamp recorded.\n- For unlocking : \n    - if purchase finalized, reserved -> booked, lock released.\n    - if abandoned, status changed back to available when expiration time reached, and lock released.\n    - To handle expiration time, we use a cron job to periodically query for rows with \"reserved\" status and expiration time reached, and set them back to \"available\"\n\nProblems:\n    - Delay in unlocking: there's a delay between ticket expiration and cron job trigger, this is not good specially for popular events and seat remains un-available even after expiration time till the cron resets it reducing booking opportunities.\n    - Reliability Issue : If cron job fails or has delays, more issues then, poor customer experience, revenue loss.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "WZOfmt6zfp4nYRfHzH_TI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2031.94048841404,
      "y": 1640.4674089924592,
      "width": 1547.759033203125,
      "height": 200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aC",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1904250512,
      "version": 759,
      "versionNonce": 353523344,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783489863903,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3. Great Solution : Implicit Status with Status Expiration Time\n- Instead of relying on cron, we enhance our view event query on tickets\n- So, instead of just querying for \"available\" tickets we search for \"available\" + \"reserved\"(but expiration time reached) and update it with new expiration time.\n\nProblems:\n    - Read operations are going to be slightly slow, as filter on two values required now.\n    - Could be optimized using materializd views or compound indexe.\n    - Also have a cron job for periodic sweeping, but our new way of searching does not affect if sweep is delayed.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3. Great Solution : Implicit Status with Status Expiration Time\n- Instead of relying on cron, we enhance our view event query on tickets\n- So, instead of just querying for \"available\" tickets we search for \"available\" + \"reserved\"(but expiration time reached) and update it with new expiration time.\n\nProblems:\n    - Read operations are going to be slightly slow, as filter on two values required now.\n    - Could be optimized using materializd views or compound indexe.\n    - Also have a cron job for periodic sweeping, but our new way of searching does not affect if sweep is delayed.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
