      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "4. Great Solution : Distributed Lock with TTL\n- Implement a distributed lock with a TTL using Redis.\n- Redis is in-memory, lock acquisition and release are extremely fast under high concurrency.\n- When ticket selected, acquire lock in Redis(ticketId) with predefined TTL\n- If purchase completed, DB status updated \"available\" -> \"booked\" and redis-lock manually released by application before TTL.\n- If TTL expires(user abandons, payment failure etc.), redis automatically releases the lock, ensuring ticket becomes immediately available.\n- Only 2 status to maintain for Ticket in DB available and booked.\n- Redis's SET key value NX EX seconds command is atomic, only one client is able to access.\n- for Multi-ticket booking, acquire locks sequentially per ticket, if any lock fails, release all you acquired.\n- Using a Lua script on Redis can make multi-lock acquisition atomic if tickets hash to same Redis node.\n\nProblems:\n    - Read-path complexity: with reservation living on Redis rather on DB, event service needs a way to show reserved seats as unavailable on Seat Map\n      -> query redis for all locked ticket IDs for a given event (using Redis Set keyed by event:{eventId}:reserved)\n          this adds a round trip to read path but is fast in practice\n      -> write-through a \"reserved\" status to DB when acquiring lock, and treat redis's TTL as source of truth and use periodic sweep in DB\n    - Failure Handling: If our lock goes down, so for that period of time our experience is degraded. We won't have double booking as at DB level we use OCC(Optimistic Concurrency Control) or row-level locking.\n      downside is user can get error after filling our payment details and someone else beats them to it. But its better that having all tickets appear unavailable(as would be the case if our cron job failed in previous sol.)\n    - TTL expiration during payment: TTL(10mins) and payment completed in (11mins) and some other user books in that 1 min we need to refund the first user for failed booking. Set the good TTL to minimise this or consider extending the lock if payment is initiated.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "4. Great Solution : Distributed Lock with TTL\n- Implement a distributed lock with a TTL using Redis.\n- Redis is in-memory, lock acquisition and release are extremely fast under high concurrency.\n- When ticket selected, acquire lock in Redis(ticketId) with predefined TTL\n- If purchase completed, DB status updated \"available\" -> \"booked\" and redis-lock manually released by application before TTL.\n- If TTL expires(user abandons, payment failure etc.), redis automatically releases the lock, ensuring ticket becomes immediately available.\n- Only 2 status to maintain for Ticket in DB available and booked.\n- Redis's SET key value NX EX seconds command is atomic, only one client is able to access.\n- for Multi-ticket booking, acquire locks sequentially per ticket, if any lock fails, release all you acquired.\n- Using a Lua script on Redis can make multi-lock acquisition atomic if tickets hash to same Redis node.\n\nProblems:\n    - Read-path complexity: with reservation living on Redis rather on DB, event service needs a way to show reserved seats as unavailable on Seat Map\n      -> query redis for all locked ticket IDs for a given event (using Redis Set keyed by event:{eventId}:reserved)\n          this adds a round trip to read path but is fast in practice\n      -> write-through a \"reserved\" status to DB when acquiring lock, and treat redis's TTL as source of truth and use periodic sweep in DB\n    - Failure Handling: If our lock goes down, so for that period of time our experience is degraded. We won't have double booking as at DB level we use OCC(Optimistic Concurrency Control) or row-level locking.\n      downside is user can get error after filling our payment details and someone else beats them to it. But its better that having all tickets appear unavailable(as would be the case if our cron job failed in previous sol.)\n    - TTL expiration during payment: TTL(10mins) and payment completed in (11mins) and some other user books in that 1 min we need to refund the first user for failed booking. Set the good TTL to minimise this or consider extending the lock if payment is initiated.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "lyBOganMt-2nI_P_jdyNV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2057.795584759024,
      "y": 2444.556007271684,
      "width": 3547.456787109375,
      "height": 150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aE",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1450364016,
      "version": 91,
      "versionNonce": 1484360816,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783491421868,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Two important steps during booking are below\n- The user fills out their payment details on the checkout page. The client uses Stripe.js to tokenize the card details — our server never sees raw credit card numbers (this is standard for PCI compliance). The client sends the resulting payment token along with the bookingId to our server, which creates a Stripe PaymentIntent. \n   Stripe processes the payment and notifies our system via webhook that the payment was successful.\n- Upon successful payment confirmation from Stripe, our system's webhook retrieves the bookingId embedded within the Stripe metadata. With this bookingId, the webhook initiates a database transaction to concurrently update the Ticket and Booking tables. Specifically, the status of the ticket linked to the booking is changed to \"sold\" in the Ticket table. \n  Simultaneously, the corresponding booking entry in the Booking table is marked as \"confirmed.\" This webhook handler should be idempotent — Stripe can retry webhooks on failure, so processing the same event twice shouldn't result in duplicate state changes. \n  Using the bookingId as an idempotency key and checking the current booking status before updating ensures safe retries.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Two important steps during booking are below\n- The user fills out their payment details on the checkout page. The client uses Stripe.js to tokenize the card details — our server never sees raw credit card numbers (this is standard for PCI compliance). The client sends the resulting payment token along with the bookingId to our server, which creates a Stripe PaymentIntent. \n   Stripe processes the payment and notifies our system via webhook that the payment was successful.\n- Upon successful payment confirmation from Stripe, our system's webhook retrieves the bookingId embedded within the Stripe metadata. With this bookingId, the webhook initiates a database transaction to concurrently update the Ticket and Booking tables. Specifically, the status of the ticket linked to the booking is changed to \"sold\" in the Ticket table. \n  Simultaneously, the corresponding booking entry in the Booking table is marked as \"confirmed.\" This webhook handler should be idempotent — Stripe can retry webhooks on failure, so processing the same event twice shouldn't result in duplicate state changes. \n  Using the bookingId as an idempotency key and checking the current booking status before updating ensures safe retries.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "smm_-DyjSaRo06QhS68-w",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2076.4371129602737,
      "y": 2746.007132239309,
      "width": 2593.597900390625,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aF",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 634757744,
      "version": 1311,
      "versionNonce": 636378224,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783492309148,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to scale view API to 10 millions of concurrent requests during popular events?\n\nGreat Solution : Caching, LB and Horizontal Scaling\n- Caching : Prioritize caching of data with read rates and low update frequency like event details, performer, venue etc.\n  cache it heavily as this data doesn't changes and load on our SQL DB leading to high availability\n  use eventId:eventObject as cache key-value\n  Use Redis/Memcached as write-through\n  for cache invalidation, setup db triggers to notify caching system of data changes like event date update, performer lineup change etc., have a TTL for periodic refreshes, have composite TTL like a long one for venue info and relatively shorter for changing data\n- Load Balancing : use round-robin or Least Connections for even traffic distribution, don't show in design but good to mention.\n- Horizontal Scaling : event service is stateless, scale it to meet demans.\n\nProblems: maintaining consistency b/w DB and cache, managing large nos of instances presents complexities.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to scale view API to 10 millions of concurrent requests during popular events?\n\nGreat Solution : Caching, LB and Horizontal Scaling\n- Caching : Prioritize caching of data with read rates and low update frequency like event details, performer, venue etc.\n  cache it heavily as this data doesn't changes and load on our SQL DB leading to high availability\n  use eventId:eventObject as cache key-value\n  Use Redis/Memcached as write-through\n  for cache invalidation, setup db triggers to notify caching system of data changes like event date update, performer lineup change etc., have a TTL for periodic refreshes, have composite TTL like a long one for venue info and relatively shorter for changing data\n- Load Balancing : use round-robin or Least Connections for even traffic distribution, don't show in design but good to mention.\n- Horizontal Scaling : event service is stateless, scale it to meet demans.\n\nProblems: maintaining consistency b/w DB and cache, managing large nos of instances presents complexities.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "uINVG6S7LZ1MHMOrDBmB9",
      "type": "text",
