    {
      "id": "XGPwbAYVpKw3EpmhkPhD1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2088.0756617015095,
      "y": 3134.0522900698643,
      "width": 2680.658203125,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aH",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 856707728,
      "version": 1749,
      "versionNonce": 1299553936,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783493478240,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to ensure good user experience during high demand events will millions booking simultaneously?\n- with popular events, the loaded seat-map will go stale quickly, users grow frustrated as they repeatedly click on a seat, only to find out it has already been booked.\n- need to ensure seat map is upto date and users are notified of changes in real-time.\n\n1. Good Solution : SSE for Real-Time Seat Updates\n- use SSE to push updates to client in real-time, this allows us to update the seat map as soon as a seat is booked/reserved without refreshing the page.\n\nProblems: works for moderately popular events, but will suffer for extremely popular ones say for Taylor Swift event the seat map will immediately fill up.\npoor user experience as seats disappear in an instant.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Virtual Waiting Queue for extremely popular events\n- Maintain a virtual queue that sits in front of your booking service and users are placed in this queue before event being able to see the booking page(seat map selection).\n- This controls the flow of users accessing the booking interface, preventing system overload and enhancing the user experience.\n    1. When a user requests to view the booking page, they are placed in a virtual queue. We establish a persistent connection(SSE) with their client and add them to the queue. \n       The queue itself can be backed by Redis (using a sorted set with timestamps for ordering).\n    2. Periodically or based on certain criteria (like tickets booked), dequeue users from the front of the queue. Notify these users via their connection that they can proceed to purchase tickets.\n    3. At the same time, mark the user as \"admitted\" in Redis (e.g., add their session ID to an admitted:{eventId} set with a TTL). The Booking Service checks this set before allowing any reservation requests, rejecting users who haven't been admitted through the queue.\n\nProblems: Long wait times in wait queue, especially if estimated wait times are not accurate or if the queue moves slower than expected growing customer frustration.\nTo overcome this provide user constant feedback on their queue position and estimated wait time.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to ensure good user experience during high demand events will millions booking simultaneously?\n- with popular events, the loaded seat-map will go stale quickly, users grow frustrated as they repeatedly click on a seat, only to find out it has already been booked.\n- need to ensure seat map is upto date and users are notified of changes in real-time.\n\n1. Good Solution : SSE for Real-Time Seat Updates\n- use SSE to push updates to client in real-time, this allows us to update the seat map as soon as a seat is booked/reserved without refreshing the page.\n\nProblems: works for moderately popular events, but will suffer for extremely popular ones say for Taylor Swift event the seat map will immediately fill up.\npoor user experience as seats disappear in an instant.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Virtual Waiting Queue for extremely popular events\n- Maintain a virtual queue that sits in front of your booking service and users are placed in this queue before event being able to see the booking page(seat map selection).\n- This controls the flow of users accessing the booking interface, preventing system overload and enhancing the user experience.\n    1. When a user requests to view the booking page, they are placed in a virtual queue. We establish a persistent connection(SSE) with their client and add them to the queue. \n       The queue itself can be backed by Redis (using a sorted set with timestamps for ordering).\n    2. Periodically or based on certain criteria (like tickets booked), dequeue users from the front of the queue. Notify these users via their connection that they can proceed to purchase tickets.\n    3. At the same time, mark the user as \"admitted\" in Redis (e.g., add their session ID to an admitted:{eventId} set with a TTL). The Booking Service checks this set before allowing any reservation requests, rejecting users who haven't been admitted through the queue.\n\nProblems: Long wait times in wait queue, especially if estimated wait times are not accurate or if the queue moves slower than expected growing customer frustration.\nTo overcome this provide user constant feedback on their queue position and estimated wait time.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "5ohGjyTfi9Z0xRtRH9vDh",
