    {
      "id": "EtseO9LPfZh3Y18IDKbPA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2095.956320104657,
      "y": 3811.238077363749,
      "width": 1943.1387939453125,
      "height": 775,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aJ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1845628560,
      "version": 2130,
      "versionNonce": 696012944,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783497085848,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to improve search to ensure low latency?\n- Searches cannot be served by DB as queries like below are very slow\n    -- slow query\n        SELECT * \n        FROM Events\n        WHERE name LIKE '%Taylor%' \n          OR description LIKE '%Taylor%' \n\n1. Good Solution : Indexing & SQL Query Optimization\n- Create indexes on Event, Performer, Venues table on fields that are frequently queried eg. event name, event date, performer name, venue location.\n- To optimize queries, use techniques like EXPLAIN to analyse query execution plan, avoid SELECT *, using LIMIT to restrict nos of rows returned, using UNION instead of OR\n\nProblems\n    - Standard indexes are less effective for partial string matches(searching for Taylor instead of Taylor Swift)\n    - Indexes increase storage requirements and slow down write operations as each write needs an index update.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Full-text Indexes in the DB\n- PostgreSQL has built in full text search using tsvector and GIN indexes, and MySQL has its own full text indexing.\n- These don't use Lucene, but make queries for partial searches much faster than doing full table scan using LIKE\n\nProblems:  Full- text indexes require Additional Storage and can still be slower than standard indexes, could be difficult to maintain as they require special handling in both queries and maintaining DB\n\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Use Full-text Search Engine like ElasticSearch\n- Very fast and scales well\n- Use CDC for near real-time data sync from Postgres DB to ES, this captures DB operations like insert, updates, deletes and replicates them to ES index.\n- Enable fuzzy search functionality with ES, which allows error tolerance in search queries, this handles typos and still get matching results, would be very difficult with SQL\n\nProblems: keeping ES and Postgres in sync is bit complex, adds to infra complexity and cost.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to improve search to ensure low latency?\n- Searches cannot be served by DB as queries like below are very slow\n    -- slow query\n        SELECT * \n        FROM Events\n        WHERE name LIKE '%Taylor%' \n          OR description LIKE '%Taylor%' \n\n1. Good Solution : Indexing & SQL Query Optimization\n- Create indexes on Event, Performer, Venues table on fields that are frequently queried eg. event name, event date, performer name, venue location.\n- To optimize queries, use techniques like EXPLAIN to analyse query execution plan, avoid SELECT *, using LIMIT to restrict nos of rows returned, using UNION instead of OR\n\nProblems\n    - Standard indexes are less effective for partial string matches(searching for Taylor instead of Taylor Swift)\n    - Indexes increase storage requirements and slow down write operations as each write needs an index update.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Full-text Indexes in the DB\n- PostgreSQL has built in full text search using tsvector and GIN indexes, and MySQL has its own full text indexing.\n- These don't use Lucene, but make queries for partial searches much faster than doing full table scan using LIKE\n\nProblems:  Full- text indexes require Additional Storage and can still be slower than standard indexes, could be difficult to maintain as they require special handling in both queries and maintaining DB\n\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Use Full-text Search Engine like ElasticSearch\n- Very fast and scales well\n- Use CDC for near real-time data sync from Postgres DB to ES, this captures DB operations like insert, updates, deletes and replicates them to ES index.\n- Enable fuzzy search functionality with ES, which allows error tolerance in search queries, this handles typos and still get matching results, would be very difficult with SQL\n\nProblems: keeping ES and Postgres in sync is bit complex, adds to infra complexity and cost.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "B0ok14ixAO0x5_uWF5boG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2111.6583044145227,
      "y": 4781.739301973142,
      "width": 1741.658447265625,
      "height": 575,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aK",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2008111760,
      "version": 1282,
      "versionNonce": 150118032,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783497632598,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to speed up frequently repeated search queries and reduce load on search infra?\n\n1. Good Solution : Caching\n- Using Redis/Memcache to store results of frequently executed search queries.\n- Construct cache keys using search query params to uniquely identify each query\n- set appropriate TTL to ensure freshness.\n    {\n      \"key\": \"search:keyword=Taylor Swift&start=2021-01-01&end=2021-12-31\",\n      \"value\": [event1, event2, event3],\n      \"ttl\": 60 * 60 * 24 // 24 hours\n    }\n\nProblems: efficiently maintaining cache invalidation is challenging, stale cache can result to incorrect search result,\nbecomes more complex if you cache fuzzy search results.\nFrequent cache misses can lead to increased load of search infra during peak times.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Implement Query Result Caching and Edge Caching Techniques\n- ES has build in capabilities to store frequent query results\n- ES maintains query caches at the shard level for filter results, plus a separate shard-level request cache for caching full search responses(useful for aggregation-heavy queries)\n- Could also use CDN to cache search results geographically closer to user eg. AWS Cloudfront.\n\nSame problems as above.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to speed up frequently repeated search queries and reduce load on search infra?\n\n1. Good Solution : Caching\n- Using Redis/Memcache to store results of frequently executed search queries.\n- Construct cache keys using search query params to uniquely identify each query\n- set appropriate TTL to ensure freshness.\n    {\n      \"key\": \"search:keyword=Taylor Swift&start=2021-01-01&end=2021-12-31\",\n      \"value\": [event1, event2, event3],\n      \"ttl\": 60 * 60 * 24 // 24 hours\n    }\n\nProblems: efficiently maintaining cache invalidation is challenging, stale cache can result to incorrect search result,\nbecomes more complex if you cache fuzzy search results.\nFrequent cache misses can lead to increased load of search infra during peak times.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Implement Query Result Caching and Edge Caching Techniques\n- ES has build in capabilities to store frequent query results\n- ES maintains query caches at the shard level for filter results, plus a separate shard-level request cache for caching full search responses(useful for aggregation-heavy queries)\n- Could also use CDN to cache search results geographically closer to user eg. AWS Cloudfront.\n\nSame problems as above.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
